    "body": "## Problem\n\n`*.al` is mapped unconditionally to `perl`. This follows Vim's runtime, where\n`runtime/filetype.vim` has `au BufNewFile,BufRead *.plx,*.al,*.psgi setf perl`, and the\nextension there means Perl's AutoLoader: generated per-sub chunks written by `AutoSplit`\ninto `auto/` directories.\n\nThe same extension is used by AL, the language of Microsoft Dynamics 365 Business Central.\nAL is a recognised language in GitHub Linguist, has a maintained tree-sitter grammar, and\naccounts for the large majority of `.al` files written today. Business Central developers\ncurrently get `perl` filetype, and therefore Perl syntax highlighting, indentation, and\n`ftplugin` behaviour, on every file they open.\n\nPerl AutoLoader files remain in the wild, so the extension cannot simply be reassigned.\n\n## Solution\n\nDetect the two by content, following the pattern already used for other ambiguous\nextensions such as `*.cls` and `*.bas`.\n\nA file is `al` if either of the following appears in the first 200 lines:\n\n* an AL object declaration at the start of a line, meaning one of the object kinds\n  (`codeunit`, `page`, `table`, `tableextension`, `report`, `xmlport`, `query`, `enum`,\n  `interface`, `permissionset`, and so on) followed by whitespace\n* a `namespace` or `using` declaration\n\nAnything else keeps returning `perl`, so existing AutoLoader users are unaffected by\ndefault. `g:filetype_al` overrules the guess, matching the convention documented under\n`filetype-overrule`.\n\n### On the keyword list\n\nThe detection deliberately requires declaration syntax rather than a bare keyword match.\nSeveral AL object kinds (`table`, `page`, `report`, `value`) are ordinary English words\nthat appear freely in Perl sources. GitHub Linguist matches those keywords anywhere in the\nfile, which is acceptable when the cost of a false positive is a wrong colour in a language\nbar, but not when the cost is a Perl user losing their filetype.\n\nAs a concrete example, this file is correctly detected as `perl`:\n\n```perl\nsub value {\n  my $table = shift;\n  # report page interface\n}\n```\n\n### Measurements\n\nRun against two corpora:\n\n| Corpus | Files | Correctly detected |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Business Central application sources | 15358 | 99.609% |\n| Perl AutoLoader files shipped with Git for Windows | 75 | 100% |\n\nThe 60 AL files not detected contain no object declaration at all. Their entire contents\nare commented out behind `#if not CLEAN26`, so there is nothing to match, and they fall\nback to `perl`.\n\nTreating AL preprocessor directives (`#if`, `#pragma`, `#region`) as an additional signal\nwas tried and rejected: it recovered 13 of those files but introduced 3 false positives on\nPerl sources, where lines such as `#if what we've read so far is greater or equal` are\nordinary comments.\n\n### Tests\n\n`Test_al_file()` covers an object declaration, a namespace and using preamble, a Perl\nAutoLoader chunk, and the `g:filetype_al` override. `file.al` is removed from the `perl`\nentry in `s:filename_checks`, since detection is now content-based.\n\n### Note on Vim parity\n\nVim has the same mapping at `runtime/filetype.vim:865`. I intend to send an equivalent\nchange there so the two runtimes do not drift.\n",
